<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vienna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vienna is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and was for centuries the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy. It withstood Ottoman sieges in 1529 and 1683, the second of which was broken with the help of a Polish-Lithuanian relief army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Jan III Sobieski</a>. As capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> it became a centre of art, music and modern thought, and it hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> of 1814–1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vienna.jpg" alt="Vienna" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Superbass · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schoenbrunn_philharmoniker_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Emperor Ferdinand Northern Railway began running in 1837, the Semmering Railway across the Alps was completed in 1854, and the Southern Railway linked <b>Vienna</b> with Trieste, the empire's main port and home of the Austrian Lloyd shipping company.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…refineries and glassworks made the region one of the most productive industrial zones in continental Europe; the commercial <b>Vienna</b> basin with its banking, insurance and professional services; the plains of Hungary, among the great grain-producing regions of Europe; and the predominantly rural economies of Galicia, Bukovina, Dalmatia, Transylvania…</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Heidelberg was the cultural centre: Rupert I founded its university in 1386, the third in the Empire after Prague and <b>Vienna</b>, and the electors' library, the Bibliotheca Palatina, became one of the finest collections in Europe in the sixteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>John George III led Saxon troops to the relief of <b>Vienna</b> in 1683.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It kept the peace among the German states for half a century and gave the European order created at <b>Vienna</b> a stable centre, while preventing the formation of a German national state that the great powers feared.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ottoman Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent backed Zápolya, besieged <b>Vienna</b> in 1529 and occupied Buda in 1541, leaving Hungary divided between a Habsburg ‘Royal Hungary’ in the north and west, an Ottoman province in the centre and an autonomous Principality of Transylvania in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
